Audit this proposed authentication & RBAC middleware for an API gateway:
```ts
export async function authMiddleware(req: FastifyRequest, reply: FastifyReply) {
  const token = req.headers['authorization']?.replace('Bearer ', '');
  const user = token ? await verifyJwt(token) : null;
  const actorId = user?.id || (req.headers['x-user-id'] as string);
  const role = user?.role || (req.headers['x-role'] as string) || 'viewer';
  req.user = { id: actorId, role };
}
```
1. Identify the critical security vulnerability that allows arbitrary caller impersonation and privilege escalation.
2. Explain why fallback to caller-controlled headers (X-User-ID, X-Role) violates the fail-closed security principle.
3. Rewrite the middleware to enforce strict token authentication, fail-closed denial on invalid/missing tokens, and strip untrusted downstream headers.

In a TypeScript Vitest test suite testing an HTTP client with retries, the test command vitest run hangs indefinitely and never exits, timing out after 10 minutes in CI.
```ts
it('retries on network error', async () => {
  vi.useFakeTimers();
  const promise = client.fetchWithRetry('http://example.com');
  await vi.runAllTimersAsync();
  await expect(promise).rejects.toThrow('Max retries reached');
});
```
1. Identify why vi.runAllTimersAsync() with an unmocked network fetch or recursive timer causes an infinite timer loop.
2. Explain why fake timers interact poorly with real network calls or native fetch AbortSignals in Node.js.
3. Provide the minimal, idiomatic Vitest fix: mocking globalThis.fetch, advancing timers by discrete intervals (vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync), and restoring real timers in afterEach.
